about performance management, the system that pervades much of the organizational world,

4
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:35,740
and yet fails to actually enhance performance. I shared the six universal truths of great

5
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:43,880
performance, and also how we can pay people without performance ratings. If you haven't

6
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:52,680
got a chance to check those out, do take a look at episodes 11, 12, and 13. Today, I focus on

7
00:00:52,680 --> 00:01:03,420
truth number six. How well-being drives great performance. Watch on to learn more. And first,

8
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:13,720
do like and subscribe. Conventional management practices focus on driving hard, driving more

9
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:21,300
efficiency, driving people longer and harder over a period of time. And the belief is that the more

10
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:28,940
and harder you drive, the better the performance. This works for some people and for some of the time,

11
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:40,220
but in the long run, this is a recipe for creating stress, illness, ill will, and strife. Eventually,

12
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:49,480
it harms the very people that create great outcomes. It is one thing to deliver high performance for a

13
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:58,760
quarter or two or for a year or two. But if one seeks to have high performance over a long period of time,

14
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:06,280
we need to seek inspiration elsewhere. We've got to learn from human evolution how tribes and

15
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:14,620
civilizations actually lasted over long periods of time and conquered great adversity. And also,

16
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:22,320
from the most modern science and the developments that tell us about the human body and how we can

17
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:28,460
create good outcomes. We've been looking to do precisely that. And in seeking that inspiration,

18
00:02:28,460 --> 00:02:37,160
we have learned that there are four principles for sustainable high performance. The first principle

19
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:47,960
is a simple one. Healthy habits, adequate sleep, good nutrition, and reasonable exercise. Those are the key

20
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:57,620
to being able to show up every single day and do our very best. Second, autonomy, which simply means

21
00:02:57,620 --> 00:03:06,760
having the flexibility to be able to lead our lives outside of work alongside spending the many hours that we do

22
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:16,280
at work. And the second aspect of autonomy is latitude. Either being told what to do or how to do it, but not both.

23
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:26,160
So we can put our own unique signature on our work. Third, purpose. Knowing that what we are doing is

24
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:34,440
actually about something bigger than any one of us. Where the work that we do is valuable to others

25
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:43,640
and to the society is crucial to well-being and to performance. And finally, number four, a sense of

26
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:53,240
belonging. When we know that we are part of a community, of a tribe, of a group with our own unique identity,

27
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:59,960
but with a shared sense of mission and where there is safety, both physical and psychological,

28
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:09,160
of being part of this group where we will take care of each other, that creates an environment and a sense of

29
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:17,160
belonging which is so crucial to sustainable performance. When these four conditions are met, healthy habits,

30
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:25,960
autonomy, purpose, and belonging, we lay the foundations and edifice for creating sustainable high performance.

31
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:34,360
Those organizations that will outlast others over a long period of time and where people can lead

32
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:41,400
healthy lives, fulfilling lives, and be proud of what they actually create for the world at large.

33
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:43,160
Thanks so much for tuning in.

34
